.\"
.\" MQ_OPEN(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH MQ_OPEN 2 "2026-07-01" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
mq_open, mq_close, mq_unlink, mq_timedsend, mq_timedreceive, mq_notify,
mq_getsetattr \- POSIX message-queue system calls (native personality)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/syscall.h>
.B #include <mqueue.h>
.PP
.BI "int     syscall(SYS_MQ_OPEN, const char *" name ", int " oflag ", mode_t " mode ", struct mq_attr *" attr );
.BI "int     syscall(SYS_MQ_CLOSE, int " mqd );
.BI "int     syscall(SYS_MQ_UNLINK, const char *" name );
.BI "int     syscall(SYS_MQ_TIMEDSEND, int " mqd ", const char *" msg ", size_t " len ", unsigned " prio ", const struct timespec *" abstime );
.BI "ssize_t syscall(SYS_MQ_TIMEDRECEIVE, int " mqd ", char *" msg ", size_t " len ", unsigned *" prio ", const struct timespec *" abstime );
.BI "int     syscall(SYS_MQ_NOTIFY, int " mqd ", const struct sigevent *" sev );
.BI "int     syscall(SYS_MQ_GETSETATTR, int " mqd ", const struct mq_attr *" newattr ", struct mq_attr *" oldattr );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the native Substrate system calls that back the POSIX message-queue
library functions in librt (see
.BR mq_open (3)).
POSIX message queues are named, cross-process kernel objects held in a fixed
table (see
.BR sys/kern/posix_mqueue.c ).
Application code normally uses the librt wrappers rather than invoking these
directly; the numbers are assigned in
.BR <arch/i386/syscall.h> :
.B SYS_MQ_OPEN
(409),
.B SYS_MQ_UNLINK
(410),
.B SYS_MQ_TIMEDSEND
(411),
.B SYS_MQ_TIMEDRECEIVE
(412),
.B SYS_MQ_NOTIFY
(413),
.B SYS_MQ_GETSETATTR
(414), and
.B SYS_MQ_CLOSE
(415).
.PP
.B SYS_MQ_OPEN
creates or opens the queue
.I name
(a leading-slash POSIX name) and returns an opaque message-queue descriptor
.RB ( mqd_t )
drawn from a per-open kernel table.  With
.B O_CREAT
the
.I attr
argument supplies
.I mq_maxmsg
and
.IR mq_msgsize ;
a NULL
.I attr
selects the kernel defaults.  The descriptor encodes a slot index and a
sequence number so a stale (closed and reused) descriptor is rejected.
.PP
.B SYS_MQ_CLOSE
releases the descriptor's reference; the queue is destroyed once its last
descriptor closes and its name has been unlinked.
.B SYS_MQ_UNLINK
removes the name (and wakes any thread blocked on the queue, which then fails
with
.BR EIDRM ).
.PP
.B SYS_MQ_TIMEDSEND
and
.B SYS_MQ_TIMEDRECEIVE
enqueue and dequeue a message.  Messages are ordered by decreasing priority,
FIFO within a priority.  A blocking call honours
.B O_NONBLOCK
.RB ( EAGAIN )
and, when
.I abstime
is non-NULL, an absolute
.B CLOCK_REALTIME
deadline
.RB ( ETIMEDOUT );
a NULL
.I abstime
blocks indefinitely.  Blocking uses an interruptible sleep queue
.RB ( EINTR ).
.PP
.B SYS_MQ_NOTIFY
registers or clears a single-shot notification (see
.BR mq_notify (3)).
.B SYS_MQ_GETSETATTR
retrieves the queue attributes and, when
.I newattr
is non-NULL, updates the per-descriptor
.B O_NONBLOCK
flag; the previous attributes are stored in
.I oldattr
if non-NULL.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The kernel returns a non-negative value on success (a descriptor for
.BR SYS_MQ_OPEN ,
a byte count for
.BR SYS_MQ_TIMEDRECEIVE ,
0 otherwise) or a negative
.I errno
on failure.  The librt wrappers translate the negative value into the POSIX
.B \-1
+
.I errno
convention.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
The caller lacks permission for the queue.
.TP
.B EAGAIN
A non-blocking send/receive could not proceed (queue full/empty).
.TP
.B EBADF
The descriptor is invalid or not open in the required direction.
.TP
.B EEXIST
.B O_CREAT | O_EXCL
was given for a queue that already exists.
.TP
.B EIDRM
The queue was unlinked while the call was blocked.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal interrupted a blocked call.
.TP
.B EINVAL
A malformed name, invalid attributes, or an out-of-range priority.
.TP
.B EMSGSIZE
The message length exceeds the queue's
.IR mq_msgsize .
.TP
.B ENOENT
The queue does not exist and
.B O_CREAT
was not given.
.TP
.B ETIMEDOUT
An absolute timeout expired.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mq_open (3),
.BR mq_send (3),
.BR mq_receive (3),
.BR mq_notify (3),
.BR mq_getattr (3)
.SH NOTES
These entry points exist only in the native Substrate personality.
